            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>What's Happened This Week</h2>
<p>This week, while serving our clients, the Yiyu team repeatedly heard a common frustration from campaign managers: the more accounts they open, the harder it becomes to manage payments, reconciliation, and alerts manually. At month-end, teams often discover uneven balances across accounts, with critical accounts suddenly hitting spending limits and causing campaign disruptions.</p>
<p>This "account fragmentation" problem fundamentally stems from insufficient management granularity. With 5 or fewer accounts, Excel sheets and manual tracking may suffice. But once teams expand to 15, 20, or more accounts, tiered management shifts from "nice to have" to "must have."</p>
<h2>Three Signals That Tiering Is No Longer Optional</h2>
<p>This week's industry developments reveal three critical signals:</p>
<p><strong>Signal 1: Platform policies tightening, single-account limits more stringent</strong></p>
<p>A utility app team reported that their primary market's Meta ad account monthly spending cap was reduced by 30% after platform reassessment. Relying on a single "main account" for majority spend is extremely risky. But with parallel multi-account operations, how do you ensure reasonable budget allocation and payment timing across each? The answer is tiering.</p>
<p><strong>Signal 2: Payment costs rising, fragmented payments losing cost-effectiveness</strong></p>
<p>During discussions with a cross-border e-commerce team this week, they mentioned managing 12 ad accounts across different product lines and regional markets. When making payments, they found that managing each account separately created high labor costs, with occasional payment delays and inconsistent arrival times affecting campaign schedules.</p>
<p><strong>Signal 3: Financial reconciliation pressure peaking</strong></p>
<p>As month-end approached, Yiyu received multiple "urgent reconciliation" requests this week. A gaming company's month-end review revealed that spending data, payment records, and refund adjustments across different accounts had become hopelessly mixed, with manual consolidation taking two full days and prone to errors.</p>
<h2>Three Core Principles for Account Tiering</h2>
<p>Based on this week's observations, multi-account teams should establish a tiering framework based on these principles:</p>
<p><strong>1. Tier by Campaign Priority</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core accounts</strong>: Main accounts contributing 60%+ of daily spend, with dedicated payment budgets and priority monitoring alerts</li>
<li><strong>Testing accounts</strong>: For creative testing and new market exploration, lower spend but requiring flexibility</li>
<li><strong>Backup accounts</strong>: For emergency activation when core accounts hit limits, maintaining low balance</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Tier by Financial Attributes</strong></p>
<p>Group accounts by payment cycles and billing types. For example, separate prepaid from postpay accounts, with prepaid accounts on fixed weekly payment schedules and postpay accounts settled统一结算. One team that implemented this logic saw reconciliation time drop from 2 days monthly to just 3 hours.</p>
<p><strong>3. Tier by Responsibility</strong></p>
<p>In larger teams where different product lines or markets are managed by different specialists, account permissions and financial responsibilities should be clearly delineated. Avoid the chaos of "everyone can pay, no one owns the balance."</p>
<h2>This Week's Recommendation: Start with Account Health Assessment and Priority Ranking</h2>
<p>No need to rebuild all accounts at once. Start with three tasks this week:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inventory existing account status</strong>: Which accounts have had zero spend in 30 days? Which accounts consistently maintain balances below $500?</li>
<li><strong>Define tiering criteria</strong>: Label each account based on spending contribution, historical stability, and campaign objectives</li>
<li><strong>Establish minimum viable monitoring</strong>: At minimum, set up payment alerts and balance notifications to prevent campaign disruptions from insufficient funds</li>
</ul>
<p>Account tiering isn't a massive project, but early implementation yields early benefits. Among Yiyu's clients this week, teams that implemented tiering first are already experiencing tangible improvements in reconciliation efficiency and campaign stability.</p>
